WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.313 --> 00:00:05.730
ADRIAN GRAHAM:
Welcome to Building

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.550
a Virtual Post-Productoin
Suite with Google Cloud.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.250
My name's Adrian Graham, and
I'm a cloud solutions architect

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.970
for media and entertainment.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.210
And I'm also here with
my colleague Buzz Hays,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.730
who's a global lead for
industry solutions for media

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.390
and entertainment.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.690
We're going to show you
a deployment today that

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.490
involves content
creation on the cloud,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.865
and we're going
to talk about how

00:00:24.865 --> 00:00:26.910
to migrate your entire
workload to the cloud

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.120
to create an entire facility,
a virtual facility, that

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.380
can span the globe.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.960
I'm also going to
bring in Buzz Hays

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.960
right now to talk about some
of the business use cases

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.350
and some of the complexities
around building this facility.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.542
Buzz?

00:00:41.542 --> 00:00:42.750
BUZZ HAYS: Thank you, Adrian.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.330
So as you can imagine, studios
are under a lot of pressure

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.780
these days to create content
under deadlines, certainly,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.650
but also with significant
cost constraints on it,

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.650
and the quality bar keeps
going up and up and up.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.660
The audience demand
for quality content

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.940
is going through
the roof, and so we

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.200
need to find ways to support
anyone who is creating content

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.670
with the resources
they need to be

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.070
able to deliver this content.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.970
Because we're an increasingly
globalized workforce,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.590
we're looking to try and
tie together resources

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.440
from all over the world, which
presents its own challenges as

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.347
well.

00:01:17.347 --> 00:01:19.680
And by having an inflexible
infrastructure, for example,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.640
that would require people to
be on prem for any given task

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.330
is really quite restrictive.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:28.380
So what we're looking at in
the grand scheme of things

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.390
is the ability to transform
the infrastructure required

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.520
by using what we refer to as the
virtual post-production suite

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.530
in this case.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:42.720
So by taking advantage of
what we know about cloud

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.410
and looking at existing
architectures of the way

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.160
people currently do
things, we can come up

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.365
with optimal solutions.

00:01:48.365 --> 00:01:49.740
As you can imagine,
the schedules

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.510
are quite tight for
delivering content these days,

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.265
and resources can
be very constrained

00:01:54.265 --> 00:01:55.890
for no particular
reason other than you

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.270
have to get a
project out the door,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.187
and you may not have
enough compute resources,

00:01:59.187 --> 00:02:02.310
so you have to find some
alternative for that.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.560
Because profit margins
are so thin, typically,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.030
on productions,
there's not really

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.060
much time or money available
for research and development

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.830
to find new ways to
do things on prem,

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.610
so taking advantage
of cloud is definitely

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.590
something to think about.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.270
With talent shortages
the way they are,

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:21.060
talent being dispersed
all over the planet,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.083
sometimes finding a way to get
these people to collaborate

00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:26.250
when they're in disparate
locations around the world

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.230
becomes its own
set of challenges.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:32.400
And because of the high cost
of keeping hardware and people

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.980
on prem, there are a
lot of considerations

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.680
that have to be
taken into account

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.430
when you're trying to
budget appropriately

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.640
for a film or a TV show,
especially because, again,

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.145
these margins are so low.

00:02:43.145 --> 00:02:44.520
And then one of
the things that's

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.180
missing from any of these
particular workflows

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.005
is the ability to capture
the actual workflow itself.

00:02:51.005 --> 00:02:52.380
So that's what we
talk about when

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.447
we talk about this idea of a
virtual studio environment,

00:02:55.447 --> 00:02:57.030
where the production
company of record

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.550
owns all the resources
required to make a production,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.160
from inception all the
way through the archive.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.840
And as part of that,
we are archiving

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.240
the workflow, which is something
that's been missing up to now.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.280
So as we look forward, there
are different applications

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.160
for using cloud
resources, and they

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.530
apply to virtually every
department on a project.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.690
So as you can imagine,
when a project starts,

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.520
it's typically in
a business office,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.980
where they're starting with
the production administration

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:23.710
side of things.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.393
It eventually will
step into a mode

00:03:26.393 --> 00:03:28.560
where you're working with
artists in a pre-vis mode,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.303
so they start to need different
compute resources for that.

00:03:32.303 --> 00:03:34.470
Editorial is probably the
longest phase of any given

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.470
production, so it will
start early and end last,

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.660
and often is the
repository of all

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.340
of the visual and
audio information

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.050
that comes through
a production, so it

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.610
becomes sort of a central hub.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.010
That's a very important
thing to acknowledge.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.370
Visual effects-- because
there's so many visual effects

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.620
artist in the world contributing
to movies these days,

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.330
there are assets that are
all over the place that

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.955
need to be centralized
in order to make

00:03:55.955 --> 00:03:57.355
that a more efficient process.

00:03:57.355 --> 00:03:59.730
And then as we start to finish
these things through color

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.770
correction and
finishing, we need

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.560
resources to be able to pull
down these very high resolution

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.988
files to be able to work on
in real time, essentially.

00:04:07.988 --> 00:04:09.780
Sound post-production
is another area where

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.190
we can take advantage of cloud.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.047
And certainly the mastering
and the versioning--

00:04:14.047 --> 00:04:16.380
there are so many different
versions of any given titles

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.029
these days, it's
important to leverage

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:20.380
as many parallel
resources as possible.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.150
Again, a great use of cloud.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.000
So when we talk about
provisioning of a studio,

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.000
or in this case, a virtual
post-production suite

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.100
in the cloud, it starts
with workstations.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.290
Those workstations can
have very different tasks.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.540
Some can be just
spreadsheets for handling

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.920
budgets and schedules.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.262
Others start to
use visual tools.

00:04:37.262 --> 00:04:39.720
Today, we're going to be talking
about some creative visual

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:40.890
tools.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.500
And then once you start bringing
the editorial component,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.760
you have editing software.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.940
You have all these
visual and audio assets

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:52.290
that have to be maintained, so
that requires a lot of storage.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.122
Renders come from
all different places.

00:04:55.122 --> 00:04:56.580
There are trans
codes that are just

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.010
a way to get stuff into the
right form to work with.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.302
But then there's also all
the visual effects rendering,

00:05:01.302 --> 00:05:04.150
so we need an infrastructure
that's scalable for that.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.000
We also need to
be able to control

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.370
where all these assets
are, where they live,

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:11.160
and who they belong to, who has
permission for these things.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.050
So centralizing asset
management means

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.450
that, essentially, you have
a single source of truth

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:19.290
in cloud, where no one who
isn't supposed to have access

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.410
has access, and everyone who
is supposed to have access

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.140
has the proper access, but
the management of those assets

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.250
is centralized.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.890
And finally, we deal with
the archive as something

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.870
from the very beginning
of a project, where

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.500
we want to make sure that we
know, when assets are created,

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.780
where do they belong in
the grand scheme of things,

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.610
ending up with the archive, so
that any part of the process,

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.810
including the workflow,
can be recreated.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:47.150
Now, as we look at the
different types of resources

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:48.893
available to folks
to use, we really

00:05:48.893 --> 00:05:50.810
have to think about the
people, the teams that

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:52.140
are working with this stuff.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:55.770
So it's important to build
virtual workstations that

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.100
serve the need of the
artist that's using

00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.563
this particular workstation.

00:05:59.563 --> 00:06:00.980
We need to be able
to then deliver

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.980
these workstations to
wherever the people happen

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.108
to be in the world.

00:06:04.108 --> 00:06:05.900
And as part of that,
we need to orchestrate

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.380
how all these things
talk to each other,

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.050
the networking involved, the
security involved, the storage,

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:12.860
all of that, especially when
these resources are sprinkled

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.220
across the planet.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.820
So we need reliable storage.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.645
We need the ability to
scale up render resources

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:21.020
as we need them,
and we shouldn't

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.083
have to pay for them if
we're not using them.

00:06:23.083 --> 00:06:24.500
And we need the
ability to archive

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.500
this entire infrastructure
at the end of a production,

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.740
so the way we look at this is
kind of in two different skews.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:32.510
One is more the
enterprise level,

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:36.200
where we're talking about
companies that may have

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.420
a number of editing suites.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:40.670
Each suite is assigned
a given production,

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.170
and those assets
are then deployed

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:45.010
to that particular suite, but
they come from a common set.

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.750
You can think of broadcasters
as a good example of this.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:50.210
But there are other,
more siloed, productions,

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.060
where a feature film or
an independent production

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.680
doesn't need access
to any resources

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:55.700
from any other projects.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:56.922
They only require their own.

00:06:56.922 --> 00:06:58.880
So this idea of a virtual
post-production suite

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.030
in the virtual studio applies to
either of these two situations.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.960
Now, as we look at how we
connect all of these folks

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.197
from around the world
and all these resources,

00:07:10.197 --> 00:07:12.530
it's important to note that
Google is a private network,

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:15.310
so if you have all of these
people and resources that

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.500
need access to their
materials, they're

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.030
all on the same
network, no matter

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:20.647
where they happen
to be on the planet,

00:07:20.647 --> 00:07:22.480
and that's a really
important consideration.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:24.790
It's as if they're all a
very large building together,

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:27.400
and they have access to
the resources they need.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:33.050
Now, security is
something that always

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:36.870
has to be mentioned when it
comes to any form of cloud,

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:38.300
whether it's compute or storage.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.040
But when it comes to
the media industry,

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.720
they're very
sensitive, obviously,

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:44.750
to the security
aspects of the media.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.520
So we take that very seriously.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.730
We're part of all the major
industry organizations.

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:50.480
We're part of the
trusted partner network.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:53.210
We work with the ISC
and the Motion Picture

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:54.170
Association of America.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.060
We go through regular audits.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:57.560
We really understand
that security

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:00.680
is paramount in terms of how
these productions get done

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.720
and the level of secrecy
involved in keeping

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.490
these things under
wraps until such time

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.420
as they make it
out to the world.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:10.170
So we take this idea of
security extremely seriously,

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:12.620
and we have a number of
rendering workload solutions

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:15.705
that you can find on the Google
website that would help point

00:08:15.705 --> 00:08:16.830
you in the right direction.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.290
But again, we take security
very, very seriously,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.770
especially when we
start to distribute

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.590
the resources around the world.

00:08:26.768 --> 00:08:29.060
So with that, I would like
to turn this over to Adrian,

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.370
who's going to talk a little bit
about a specific implementation

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:32.990
of the virtual
post-production suite

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:34.549
and how all the
pieces fit together.

00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:36.530
Adrian?

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:37.970
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Thanks, Buzz.

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:41.169
I'm going to take us through
an example architecture of what

00:08:41.169 --> 00:08:44.150
a virtual post-production suite
might-- some of the components

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:46.940
that it's built out
of, and then we're

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:50.390
going to show one in action.

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:53.360
But I want to start by talking
about how to gain access

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.320
to our resources.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:58.340
And you can do that from any
type of workstation or laptop

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:01.970
or some sort of a hardware
client or a thin client,

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:06.302
and this could be a Windows,
Mac, Linux, Chrome OS device.

00:09:06.302 --> 00:09:08.510
It doesn't matter because
you're just viewing pixels,

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:11.330
and the pixels are streaming
in a particular format.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:13.260
We'll talk about
that in a second.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:16.910
But to get into our network, to
get into Google Cloud platform,

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:20.293
you want to be able to securely
connect to the resources.

00:09:20.293 --> 00:09:21.710
And to do that,
we're using what's

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:23.300
called a connection broker.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:25.520
And the connection
broker is very important

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:27.320
because it serves
two main purposes.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.630
Number one, it
authenticates the user.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:33.110
It provides connectivity
to an identity service

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:37.760
such as IP with G Suite or
some sort of Active Directory

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:38.570
service.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:40.910
And it can also drive
things like multi-factor

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:44.030
authentication to send a push
notification, for example,

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:47.720
to a mobile device to
authenticate a user.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:52.070
And the other main function that
the connection broker performs

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:55.760
is matching users to
resources, so when resources

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.890
are assigned to a user,
the connection broker

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.290
authenticates and
will connect the user

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.130
to their virtual workstation.

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:04.370
Now, what this
diagram does not show

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.050
are different regions
around the world,

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:09.620
and you could have artists
working in Los Angeles,

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:13.722
like me, or in Europe,
or in Tokyo, or anywhere

00:10:13.722 --> 00:10:15.680
in the world that there's
a global data center,

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.540
and run your
virtual workstations

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:20.270
and connect artists
in those locations

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:21.860
to the virtual workstations.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:25.250
You heard Buzz earlier speak
about regionality of where

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:26.090
artists--

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:27.590
you want to go where
the artists are

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:30.200
and take advantage of things
like production tax credits,

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:31.500
for example.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:33.530
You want to be able
to spin up a studio

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.680
where those production tax
credits or the artist pools

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:38.690
are available, and
you can do that

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:41.000
by deploying resources there
and putting a connection

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:42.620
broker there.

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:45.200
Now, once you're on your
virtual workstation,

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.030
you have access to
all the typical things

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:50.990
that you would enjoy with a
machine that's under your desk

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:52.490
or at work or at home.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:55.160
And you've got things like
shared file systems that can be

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:58.820
mounted as either Samba or NFS.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:02.420
And because they're shared, they
are available for read-write

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:06.500
to all users in a project,
users in different regions.

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:08.000
There's strategies
for synchronizing

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.370
the data between
shared file systems,

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:12.350
so there's some
patterns around that.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:14.780
You also have connectivity to
things like asset management

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:18.020
databases so that you could
centrally store and manage

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:21.500
your assets and do things
like metadata tagging on them

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:26.000
and bring the software that
you typically use on prem--

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:27.620
you could bring
that into the cloud

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:29.780
to provide asset management.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:31.430
Again, you're
providing your artists

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.200
with a familiar
environment that should

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.460
be indistinguishable
from your on prem.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.270
And finally, there strategies
around archiving and storage

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:43.560
to be able to offline,
for example, shots

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:47.960
that [INAUDIBLE] shots,
or to be able to move data

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.180
around a network.

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.970
There are some
storage strategies

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:54.620
in which you use object data--

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:57.350
or object storage
as not as archive,

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:01.590
but as a backend for a
different shared file system.

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:03.080
We can talk about some of that.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:06.290
We talk about a little
bit of that in the demo.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:08.090
But before we jump
into the demo,

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:09.620
I want to talk
about the technology

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:13.400
that we're using to stream to
the remote desktop to the end

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.850
point, to our
client, and for that,

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.640
we've chosen a technology
called PC Over IP

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.740
by a company called Teradici.

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:24.170
Now, there's a lot of different
streaming technologies

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:24.690
out there.

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.648
There's a lot of different
protocols out there,

00:12:26.648 --> 00:12:30.800
but we've chosen PC Over
IP because it provides

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.900
the highest image
fidelity in that it

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:35.190
can display lossless pixels.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:38.360
It can accurately represent
what the remote machine

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:40.380
is providing on your client.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:44.060
So it has this thing
called resolve to lossless,

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:46.820
or build to lossless,
which will provide you

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:50.390
an uncompressed view of
the pixels on your screen.

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:52.010
And then it has the
ability to stream,

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:55.040
for example, moving video
in a different codec

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:56.640
in different parts
of the screen.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.210
It also supports things like
high resolution monitors,

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:02.390
4k and above
monitors, as well as

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:04.850
arbitrary screen resolutions,
which is really important.

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:07.500
Some artists have a 2k
monitor in front of them,

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:10.380
and then an HD monitor,
a smaller HD monitor,

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.990
as sort of an accessory monitor.

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:15.030
Teradici can span
multiple monitors

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:18.530
and drive both of those at
independent resolutions,

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:20.030
as well as
independent framerates

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.130
as well, up to 60 frames
per second, for example.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:26.060
It's also very flexible when it
comes to bandwidth consumption,

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:29.840
so you can run
Teradici PC Over IP

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:33.920
over a very small
amount of bandwidth

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:38.750
If you've got minimal internet
at home, or via a phone

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:40.500
hotspot or Wi-Fi.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:42.530
And then when you're
in the office,

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:44.930
or if you're at home
with gig internet,

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:46.880
you can take advantage
of all that bandwidth

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:48.650
and get even higher quality.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:51.920
But it's totally adaptable and
has the ability to [INAUDIBLE],,

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:53.850
to do AV sync and audio sync.

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:56.010
And you'll see that
as part of the demo.

00:13:56.010 --> 00:14:00.470
And finally, it's highly secure
in that it's only transmitting

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:04.470
pixels, not your data, and those
pixels are encrypted as well.

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:07.520
Another important point
I can't stress enough

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:11.480
is that Teradici PC Over
IP supports more than just

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:12.320
keyboard and mouse.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:16.640
It supports other input devices,
such as this Wacom Centiq

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:19.040
tablet that you're going
to see me draw directly

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:21.800
on in the demo, and it is
able to connect that directly

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:23.240
to my clients.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:27.260
That streams directly into
my virtual workstation.

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:30.380
So with that, let's take
a look at how our workflow

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:32.430
would look during the demo.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:34.160
So here we are on
my Mac desktop.

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:38.280
And I'm going to log into my
virtual workstation using PC

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:39.830
Over IP client from Teradici.

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:42.965
This is a free download
from teradici.com.

00:14:42.965 --> 00:14:47.530
It runs on Mac, Windows,
Linux, Chrome OS,

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:48.950
and it is a software client.

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:51.100
There's also hardware clients.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:54.220
You can use what's called the
Zero Client or a thin client

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:57.940
to connect, and you can
connect in a few different ways

00:14:57.940 --> 00:14:59.290
depending on how you set it up.

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:01.930
We've set this up not to
connect to an IP address,

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:03.760
but to a fully
qualified domain name,

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:06.340
and our domain that we're
using Cloud DNS through

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.550
is editdemo.cloudme
diasolutions.dev.

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:16.840
And the login flow, like
we saw in the diagram,

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:21.140
will authenticate me through
the Active Directory service.

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:26.410
So I'm going to log in using
my Adrian demo account,

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:29.860
and it's going to
authenticate me using

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:31.220
multi-factor authentication.

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:35.330
So we have a Radius Duo server
set up in our project as well,

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:40.780
and I can either get a code
from Google Authenticator

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:42.972
or I can set up push
notifications, which I've

00:15:42.972 --> 00:15:44.180
set up, which is pretty neat.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:46.940
So I click Send Me a
Push, and on my phone,

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:51.100
I click, yes, it's me, and I'm
authenticated into the Cloud

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:53.090
Access Manager environment.

00:15:53.090 --> 00:15:55.120
The Cloud Access
Manager looks up

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:59.207
what desktops or resources
are assigned to my user,

00:15:59.207 --> 00:16:01.290
and I've got these three
resources assigned to me.

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:04.030
They're virtual workstations.

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:06.460
One is a virtual
workstation in Los Angeles.

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:08.210
Another one is in
a different region.

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:10.000
This one happens to be in Tokyo.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.880
And another virtual
workstation in Los Angeles.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:17.690
That's my media encoder
worker utility node.

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:20.920
So I'm going to connect to
my virtual workstation now.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:22.660
And because I've
been authenticated

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:24.400
through the Active
Directory service,

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.900
all of my user
permissions are going

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:30.850
to be matched to that
user identity in Windows.

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:33.130
So once logged into
my workstation,

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:35.170
I can see that this is
just a standard Windows

00:16:35.170 --> 00:16:38.530
workstation running
Windows Server 2019, which

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:40.750
includes Windows Server--

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:43.300
sorry Windows
Desktop Experience,

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:45.760
which gives me the
equivalent of Windows 10.

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.490
And on this Windows
workstation, I've

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.250
got mounted a cloud volume, a
Netapp cloud volume, and that's

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:52.240
mounted to the P drive.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:53.860
P stands for Projects.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:56.350
And this is a standard
[INAUDIBLE] file

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:58.900
system that's mounted
under SMB, and because it's

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:01.900
mounted under Samba, you get
all the standard permissions

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:04.450
that you would with Active
Directory assignments.

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:07.480
And files that I create
as me, as my user,

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:09.190
will be written as me.

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:10.930
It is also a shared
file system, so

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:13.089
that any other user
on another workstation

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:17.619
or on a different server,
Windows or Linux, in my project

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:21.560
can access this
cloud volume as well.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:26.230
So in my LEFC Edits folder,
I've got all my assets

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:29.150
and all of my
Premiere project data.

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:31.000
And I've got Premier
running, and you

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:36.820
can see I've got a fairly
straightforward Adobe Premiere

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:38.030
project.

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:43.810
And I've got the ability to
play and use standard JKL keys

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:46.570
to play backwards and forwards.

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:47.527
It's very responsive.

00:17:47.527 --> 00:17:49.360
All this data, by the
way, is being read off

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:52.540
of that Netapp cloud
file, or off the P drive.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:59.500
So I can do standard editing
tasks inside Premiere,

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:02.800
and I can get some pretty
good feedback here.

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:05.530
I can basically change
some of my edit cuts

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:10.920
so that I can build this
highlight reel, for example.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:13.730
And I get audio sync as well.

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.210
Teradici PC Over
IP supports what's

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:19.720
called Enhanced AV Sync,
which will make sure

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:22.930
that the audio and
the video are locked

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:27.160
and accommodate for different
bandwidths and changes

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:30.490
in your internet connectivity
and your bandwidth.

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:32.860
As bandwidth changes,
it can adapt.

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:35.980
It's also ideal for
working with things

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:40.720
like lip sync and other
audio specific, audio sync

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:43.690
sensitive workloads.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:47.980
And again, I'm running a single
monitor at HD resolution.

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:50.740
Teradici also supports
multiple monitors and multiple

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:54.700
resolutions up to 4k
and beyond depending

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:57.280
on what your settings are like
and what kind of bandwidth

00:18:57.280 --> 00:18:58.990
you have available to you.

