comes on from the screen right
and goes off on screen right.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:13.200
So at this point, I
can just save this off

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:15.910
as a PNG file with
an alpha channel

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:19.878
and send that over to
Buzz to use as a guide.

00:24:19.878 --> 00:24:21.670
And I just wanted to
be able to communicate

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:25.570
to him my thoughts on where
these elements should sit.

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:31.772
So, Buzz, let's have a look
at this in After Effects.

00:24:31.772 --> 00:24:32.980
BUZZ HAYS: Thank you, Adrian.

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:36.560
So what I have here is
another VM that's set up.

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:39.700
It's on the same network, and it
is connected to the same shared

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:41.020
file storage as Adrian.

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:43.900
So we both have access to
all of the elements that

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:45.410
are required for this project.

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:48.100
In this case, I'm going
to be using After Effects

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:51.220
to pull together various
elements to be able to create

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:53.230
a motion graphic
overlay that Adrian can

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:56.070
use in the edit in Premiere.

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:59.470
Now, as part of that, I'm going
to be using his reference.

00:24:59.470 --> 00:25:04.480
So as you saw a moment ago,
he created a reference here.

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:07.360
This gives us some indication as
to what should be on the screen

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:08.800
and where it should be.

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:10.990
I like to do a quick
comp, typically, just

00:25:10.990 --> 00:25:12.772
to see where things
fall on the screen,

00:25:12.772 --> 00:25:15.230
just make sure I'm not blocking
anything in the background.

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:17.530
So I'm going to comp
it really quickly

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:19.998
over a still frame of
that section of the video.

00:25:19.998 --> 00:25:21.790
So you can see the
picture in picture video

00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:25.090
up here in the right corner,
and then we have a text element

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:26.553
here, which says
date, but I think,

00:25:26.553 --> 00:25:28.720
making a creative decision,
I'm going to change that

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:31.060
to the player's name.

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:32.560
We've got some
indications as to how

00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:34.290
these elements are moving in.

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:36.790
We also have a stats card here
that'll appear at the bottom,

00:25:36.790 --> 00:25:40.310
and it will have an image
of the player itself.

00:25:40.310 --> 00:25:42.910
There's some indications
here of motion.

00:25:42.910 --> 00:25:45.400
Sometimes, you have to make
the decisions of the motion

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:47.980
based on the actual underlying
footage, because you'll

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:50.408
run into situations where the
motion graphic might block

00:25:50.408 --> 00:25:52.450
an important part of the
frame depending on where

00:25:52.450 --> 00:25:54.367
the camera is or where
the subject of interest

00:25:54.367 --> 00:25:55.578
is in the frame.

00:25:55.578 --> 00:25:57.620
So sometimes, we have to
make creative decisions.

00:25:57.620 --> 00:25:59.620
And in this case,
the underlying shot

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:02.950
is not long enough really
to be able to allow

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:06.340
this graphic element to move
all the way across the frame

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:07.430
and still be readable.

00:26:07.430 --> 00:26:09.160
So I've made an
executive decision

00:26:09.160 --> 00:26:11.080
to have a different
transition on that, which

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:13.110
you'll see in just a moment.

00:26:13.110 --> 00:26:18.230
So you can see, in this
Graphics Elements folder,

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:21.050
I've got a number of
graphics elements here.

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:25.810
One of them is this
logo from the LEFC

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:27.110
that we're going to use.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:30.130
We also have a headshot
of Diego Rossi,

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:33.280
and here's the element that
I created in Cinema 4D.

00:26:33.280 --> 00:26:39.340
So this is a video of the
picture in picture piece of it.

00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:43.870
And it's basically been
rendered into this frame.

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:46.550
And I've also included the
player's name below the frame.

00:26:46.550 --> 00:26:48.970
And if you notice, there's
a subtle light glint that's

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:51.338
happening across there,
especially in this section

00:26:51.338 --> 00:26:52.630
here, you'll see a light glint.

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:54.310
This is just to give
the graphic itself

00:26:54.310 --> 00:26:57.950
some life when it's on the frame
so it doesn't look too static.

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:01.330
But this will be an element
that is used to bring

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:02.650
in that picture in picture.

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:04.780
And anything you see that's
black here in the frame

00:27:04.780 --> 00:27:06.190
is actually going
to be transparent

00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:07.150
in the final composite.

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:09.820
That's important, so that
it looks nice and clean

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:11.170
when you put it together.

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:13.450
So I've pre-built
a sequence here

00:27:13.450 --> 00:27:16.180
that has all of
these elements in it.

00:27:16.180 --> 00:27:18.963
As you can see here in
the layer side of things,

00:27:18.963 --> 00:27:20.380
I've got a number
of text elements

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:23.830
that are native text
within After Effects.

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:26.530
I have the logo that we
mentioned a minute ago

00:27:26.530 --> 00:27:30.622
from the LEFC, and then I have
the headshot of the player.

00:27:30.622 --> 00:27:32.080
Have a couple of
shapes here, which

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:34.840
I'll show you in a second,
and then at the bottom here,

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:37.020
I have the motion
graphics element.

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:39.413
So as I play this in slo-mo--

00:27:39.413 --> 00:27:41.830
here, I'll just drag through
the timeline-- you'll notice,

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:44.230
as it comes in, it looks
a little bit blurry.

00:27:44.230 --> 00:27:46.945
That's because I've
added motion blur to it.

00:27:46.945 --> 00:27:48.820
Because we're dealing
with video frame rates,

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:52.820
sometimes motion graphics look
a little stuttering or jittery.

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:56.200
So the best way to counteract
that is to enable motion blur.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:59.630
And so if you look over
here in the timeline,

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:02.410
there's a little
indicator here, which

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:04.150
is the motion blur setting.

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:08.320
And it essentially emulates
what a camera shutter would do.

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:12.310
So there's a natural motion blur
that happens in normal camera,

00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:13.700
so this is emulating that.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:16.870
So I've turned this on for all
the layers, because basically,

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:18.800
all the layers are
moving at some point,

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:20.943
so that will give them
a nice smooth motion.

00:28:20.943 --> 00:28:22.610
So as you can see
again, as it moves in,

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:25.300
you see the leading edge
is a little bit blurred.

00:28:25.300 --> 00:28:27.798
And then as this graphic
element at the bottom--

00:28:27.798 --> 00:28:30.340
this is the change I made, where
it's rotating in now instead

00:28:30.340 --> 00:28:31.413
of sliding in--

00:28:31.413 --> 00:28:32.830
it has a little
bit of motion blur

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:35.230
right there at the
beginning as it comes in,

00:28:35.230 --> 00:28:38.420
and then it settles, so
it's in nice, sharp focus.

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:41.050
So now that all these
elements are together,

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:45.310
and the timing seems to fit
well, now what I'm going to do

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:48.983
is take this particular element,
and I'm going to render it out.

00:28:48.983 --> 00:28:50.650
I'm going to add it
to the render queue.

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:53.802
In this case, I'm not going
to use the Adobe Media Encoder

00:28:53.802 --> 00:28:56.260
render queue, which I could do
because that's on another VM

00:28:56.260 --> 00:28:58.760
that Adrian has set
up on this network.

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:01.530
But this is such a small
render, it's actually very quick

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:03.220
to do right here in the machine.

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:08.730
So here, I'm going to set an
output format of QuickTime,

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:13.530
and under Format, I'm going to
choose the Apple ProRes 4444

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:14.280
format.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:19.140
And the reason for that is
because not all the ProRes

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:21.020
formats support alpha channels.

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:23.850
Again, alpha channels
are the ability

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:27.720
to present this area around
the graphics as transparent.

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:31.050
If I were to render this in
the other ProRes codecs that

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:32.790
don't support alpha
channels, this

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.160
would just come out as
a giant black rectangle

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:37.890
with these elements in it, and
when I bring it into Premiere,

00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:40.390
it would obscure the
video from underneath.

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:43.590
So as long as I've chosen
alpha channel here, then

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:46.020
I will have this nice
transparent background.

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:47.650
This particular
clip has no audio.

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:49.840
So I'm just going
to turn that off.

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:54.330
And the output file
name is set down here.

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:55.270
We're all ready to go.

00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:56.640
You can see how quick
the render goes.

00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:58.057
It's going to play
it in real time

00:29:58.057 --> 00:30:00.210
here as it renders,
because again, it's

00:30:00.210 --> 00:30:01.687
a very quick render.

00:30:01.687 --> 00:30:03.270
Now, if I were doing
a lot of renders,

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:04.860
if I had a pile
of motion graphics

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:06.750
that I was going
to be doing, then I

00:30:06.750 --> 00:30:10.200
would most likely be
taking all of these renders

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:13.500
out to the media encoder so
that I could keep working.

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:16.980
By using the local queue
here in After Effects,

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:18.558
I'm tying up the
application itself,

00:30:18.558 --> 00:30:19.600
so it can't keep working.

00:30:19.600 --> 00:30:21.642
But if I were to send it
off to the render queue,

00:30:21.642 --> 00:30:22.800
I can keep working.

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:24.990
So without further ado,
I'm going to turn this over

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:28.020
to Adrian so he can take
this clip that we just

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:30.300
generated as an overlay
graphic and drop it

00:30:30.300 --> 00:30:32.040
into his edit on Premiere.

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:34.843
So thank you, and
take it away, Adrian.

00:30:34.843 --> 00:30:36.010
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Thanks, Buzz.

00:30:36.010 --> 00:30:38.440
I'm going to take those
graphics that you output

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:41.350
and drag them into my timeline.

00:30:41.350 --> 00:30:43.880
And because they're on
the shared file system,

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:46.610
we can both see the same
files and the same assets,

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:48.280
so I can just go
to that directory

00:30:48.280 --> 00:30:50.650
and grab it from
the output location.

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:53.510
I'm going to pop that in.

00:30:53.510 --> 00:30:54.820
There is the footage.

00:30:54.820 --> 00:30:55.698
It's ready to go.

00:30:55.698 --> 00:30:58.240
So I'm ready to render this,
and there's a few different ways

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:00.610
to render Adobe
Premiere projects.

00:31:00.610 --> 00:31:02.530
I could render it locally
on my workstation,

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:05.535
but I can also farm it
off to a separate node,

00:31:05.535 --> 00:31:07.660
and I've got this separate
node running Adobe Media

00:31:07.660 --> 00:31:09.858
Encoder in my same project.

00:31:09.858 --> 00:31:12.400
And I could see that here, I've
got another PC Over IP client

00:31:12.400 --> 00:31:13.570
running.

00:31:13.570 --> 00:31:16.780
So I've got it ready to go, and
it's watching a watch folder.

00:31:16.780 --> 00:31:19.880
It's looking at the Media
Encoder Transcoder folder,

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:23.140
so any file that
gets saved into there

00:31:23.140 --> 00:31:25.500
will get automatically
transcoded.

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:29.740
So I'm going to save a
copy into that location.

00:31:29.740 --> 00:31:37.120
I've got it here in my
sidebar, and save a copy there,

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:40.690
so when I go back and
watch Media Encoder,

00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:43.750
you can see it automatically
picks up that render job.

00:31:43.750 --> 00:31:47.260
And it's going to run a number
of different formats out,

00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:49.630
so I've got things
like YouTube 1080p

00:31:49.630 --> 00:31:53.570
and a low res proxy, some other
output formats here as well.

00:31:53.570 --> 00:31:58.210
So once that's done, it's
going to write the final output

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:01.250
to that same folder, but
in the Outputs folder.

00:32:01.250 --> 00:32:05.830
And we can go and look at the
results on our workstation

00:32:05.830 --> 00:32:09.790
and view the final renders in
real time with a frame viewer.

00:32:09.790 --> 00:32:15.700
So I can go into the Transcoder
folder here and go into Output,

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:20.530
and then let's look at maybe
one of the YouTube 1080p files

00:32:20.530 --> 00:32:21.530
here.

00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:23.030
This is the latest one here.

00:32:23.030 --> 00:32:26.200
I'm going to open it with DJV,
which is an open source frame

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:27.740
viewer, and there it is.

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:30.110
There's our final render.

00:32:30.110 --> 00:32:33.390
So maybe look over here.

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:36.100
There's the graphics
that we added.

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:37.740
Let's go back and
watch them come on.

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:38.810
There they go.

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:41.000
So there's the
graphics that we added.

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:43.450
So now that we've got all of
our assets written to disk,

00:32:43.450 --> 00:32:45.820
and we've got the different
formats written out,

00:32:45.820 --> 00:32:49.180
we could continue our
workflow by exporting

00:32:49.180 --> 00:32:52.780
these to object store
to connect to a CDN

00:32:52.780 --> 00:32:54.340
with one of our CDN partners.

00:32:54.340 --> 00:32:56.560
You could run them through
any number of machine

00:32:56.560 --> 00:32:59.470
learning APIs to
understand them better

00:32:59.470 --> 00:33:02.145
and be able to tag them
and monetize them properly.

00:33:04.612 --> 00:33:06.070
There's a lot of
partner technology

00:33:06.070 --> 00:33:09.820
as well that we can use
to feed these to a media

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:12.310
asset management
system, for example.

00:33:12.310 --> 00:33:14.680
Let's look at what we
saw as part of this demo.

00:33:14.680 --> 00:33:16.810
I logged in through
Active Directory using

00:33:16.810 --> 00:33:18.620
multi-factor authentication.

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:20.230
I went in through a
connection broker.

00:33:20.230 --> 00:33:22.510
The connection broker
realized I was in Los Angeles

00:33:22.510 --> 00:33:25.930
and connected me to my
workstation in Los Angeles.

00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:28.030
I was able to edit
some footage using

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:31.420
Adobe Premiere Pro with full
interactivity and very low

00:33:31.420 --> 00:33:32.620
latency.

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:35.080
I was also able to
draw on this tablet

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:37.120
and draw some notes for Buzz.

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:40.600
I handed those files over
to Buzz over our shared file

00:33:40.600 --> 00:33:43.660
system, where Buzz accessed
his own virtual workstation

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:46.930
in a similar manner, performed
activities, and then saved

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:48.880
that data back for me to grab.

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:52.930
And then finally, I rendered the
content on a separate machine

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:56.020
that is operating as my
media encoder server,

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:57.610
watching a watch
directory, and then

00:33:57.610 --> 00:34:00.760
I was able to finally take
the output video, view that,

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:03.220
scrub through it to
verify that it was OK,

00:34:03.220 --> 00:34:06.600
and all that was streaming
over Teradici PC Over IP

00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:12.219
with full video quality on,
for example, this 2k monitor,

00:34:12.219 --> 00:34:15.260
with also my Centiq
tablet attached.

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:18.310
So what we didn't have time
to show as part of this demo

00:34:18.310 --> 00:34:21.250
is a more advanced
ingestion strategy.

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:24.820
You did see me use Aspera
drive to drag that file

00:34:24.820 --> 00:34:27.639
and upload it automatically and
put that on the NetApp cloud

00:34:27.639 --> 00:34:28.550
volume.

00:34:28.550 --> 00:34:31.630
But if you have massive
amounts of data-- for example,

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:34.719
you're on a set, and you have
terabytes of data to upload--

00:34:34.719 --> 00:34:37.900
you're going to need to define
an asset ingestion strategy.

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:40.389
And there's a bunch of
technologies, both native

00:34:40.389 --> 00:34:44.080
and partner technologies on
GCP, to allow you to do that.

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:47.170
We also didn't talk about
things like disaster recovery

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:49.639
and asset management.

00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:53.239
Our workflow did not require
an asset management system,

00:34:53.239 --> 00:34:56.260
but we have a lot of ISVs,
Independent Software Vendors,

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:59.020
that we work with that
provide asset managers.

00:34:59.020 --> 00:35:03.040
There's also the notion of
disaster recovery for moving--

00:35:03.040 --> 00:35:05.790
allowing you to
operate in any region

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:09.010
in your geographic location
or in the world as part

00:35:09.010 --> 00:35:11.440
of a contingency plan.

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:14.350
I want to bring Buzz in to talk
a little bit further about how

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:18.010
to monetize and get
more out of your archive

00:35:18.010 --> 00:35:20.960
using something called
intelligent content discovery.

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:22.147
Buzz?

00:35:22.147 --> 00:35:23.230
BUZZ HAYS: Thanks, Adrian.

00:35:23.230 --> 00:35:25.450
So, yes, with intelligent
content discovery,

00:35:25.450 --> 00:35:27.370
you hear a lot about
machine learning

00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:29.140
and artificial
intelligence tools

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:31.630
that can automatically
derive metadata

00:35:31.630 --> 00:35:34.860
both on the visual content and
the audio content in files.

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:37.360
But how we use that metadata
is really important, especially

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:40.010
when it comes to monetizing
the content itself.

00:35:40.010 --> 00:35:41.635
So obviously, the
metadata is important

00:35:41.635 --> 00:35:43.927
when we're trying to find
the content while we're still

00:35:43.927 --> 00:35:46.390
working with it, while we're
producing the finished goods.

00:35:46.390 --> 00:35:50.320
But it's important to note
that the metadata comes

00:35:50.320 --> 00:35:52.090
into play when you're
actually trying

00:35:52.090 --> 00:35:53.450
to leverage the finished goods.

00:35:53.450 --> 00:35:56.260
So for example, in the use
of recommendation engines

00:35:56.260 --> 00:36:00.015
or personalization,
artificial intelligence tools

00:36:00.015 --> 00:36:01.390
that are leveraging
this metadata

00:36:01.390 --> 00:36:04.270
can actually be very
effective in the distribution

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:07.060
side of things, as
well as in the effort

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:11.110
to be able to create new
assets from the existing.

00:36:11.110 --> 00:36:13.773
So for example, if you're making
trailers or teasers or things

00:36:13.773 --> 00:36:15.940
like that, they could be
tailored to a very specific

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:19.120
demographic using
AI, for example,

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:22.070
or at least the metadata that's
associated with this content.

00:36:22.070 --> 00:36:25.570
So you can have a very
tailored view of the content

00:36:25.570 --> 00:36:29.410
as we get closer and closer
to this direct consumer world.

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:31.750
And then in terms of
regionalization, obviously

00:36:31.750 --> 00:36:33.570
as we deliver assets
these days, it

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:36.010
used to be that there was a
time delay between the North

00:36:36.010 --> 00:36:38.750
American release of something
and the European or worldwide

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:39.350
release.

00:36:39.350 --> 00:36:41.120
Now, they tend to be
day and date, which

00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:43.453
means that all of the different
versions-- and there can

00:36:43.453 --> 00:36:45.020
be literally thousands
of versions--

00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:46.740
have to be deployed
at the same time.

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:48.950
So there has to be some
automation involved

00:36:48.950 --> 00:36:51.140
in creating these
localized versions

00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:56.810
as well as in how this entire
process of localization happens

00:36:56.810 --> 00:36:58.200
and where that happens.

00:36:58.200 --> 00:37:00.530
So as Adrian
mentioned before, we

00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:04.310
